CRO of a UK building society reviewing proposed principles for a mortgage servicing contract.

**Concept and theory**

The third life-cycle stage, contracts, service level agreements (SLAs) and contract management, is described as too often a neglected key area of risk and control in third-party management. Its purpose is to remove ambiguity:

- the terms should clearly define what can and cannot be done, by whom, when and where;
- contracts define the responsibilities and expectations of both parties and establish the quality and timing of service provision (the SLA), where applicable;
- misunderstanding, or ambiguity in expectations, by either party is one of the common reasons third-party relationships fail and increase operational risk, so expectations are best set formally in the contract;
- an SLA should include, wherever possible, clear and measurable quality measures;
- contracts should be signed only after the assessment and remediation of all remaining open items or issues, and are enforceable only once signed by both parties;
- good practice is to set terms for periodic contract review and a remediation process to correct contract deficiencies.

